Marta: quick heads-up — I'm seeing intermittent `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input` in the JS SDK streaming parser when a ReadableStream chunk cuts a multi-byte char or splits a JSON object mid-object. Repro below. anyone else hit this?

Marta: repro (minimal):
```js
const r = await fetch(url);
const reader = r.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, {stream: true});
  // naive parse ----> throws when chunk is partial
  const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
  handle(obj);
  buf = '';
}
```

Sam: yup. this is classic partial-json on streaming fetch. you can't JSON.parse incremental chunks. either use newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) or an incremental parser. did you try counting braces / looking for trailing newline? :thinking_face: 

Li: fwiw, in our SDK we added a small coalescing window: buffer chunks for up to 30-80ms or until we detect a full delimiter, then flush. trades a tiny latency for reliability. fixed emoji split issues.

Marta: thanks. i tried brace counting but got tripped by arrays-of-objects and nested arrays. NDJSON would be ideal but server is currently sending bundled JSON objects.

Sam: another approach: use a tolerant streaming parser lib (e.g., https://github.com/creationix/jsonparse) that emits values as they complete. avoids manual brace counting.

Alex: micro-optimization: instead of setTimeout coalesce, try queueMicrotask/Promise.resolve to yield and let more chunks arrive without a full ms tick. less latency than setTimeout(0). example:
```js
let buffer = '';
let pending = false;
reader.read().then(onChunk);
function onChunk({done, value}){
  if(done) return flush();
  buffer += dec.decode(value, {stream: true});
  if(!pending){
    pending = true;
    queueMicrotask(()=>{ pending = false; tryParseBuffer(); });
  }
  reader.read().then(onChunk);
}
function tryParseBuffer(){
  try{ const o = JSON.parse(buffer); handle(o); buffer = ''; }
  catch(e){ /* keep buffer; wait for more */ }
}
```

Li: nice. queueMicrotask keeps it tight. we still had to handle multibyte boundary by ensuring TextDecoder used stream:true (looks like you have that). also add a fallback: if buffer grows > 1MB, reset/emit error to avoid OOM/malicious stream.

Marta: good call on the size cap. i'll add a maxBufferBytes option to the SDK and a toggle for coalescing (ms window or microtask).

DeployBot: Marta pushed branch "streaming/partial-json-fix" to origin and created PR #412 — CI queued :rocket:

Marta: thanks @DeployBot — pinging @devex for quick review on serializer changes and a note to add a unit test for emoji-splitting (use U+1F600 and multi-byte CJK example).

Alex: also add an integration test that simulates chunked sends with multibyte splits and 429/retry sequence to ensure parser resumes correctly.

Sam: if you want a super-light incremental parser we used a tiny state machine: track depth and lastNonWhitespace, and only attempt JSON.parse when depth==0 and lastChar was `}` or `]`. simpler than a full lib for our case.

Marta: perfect — i'll prototype SAM's state-machine first, fallback to jsonparse if weird cases pop up. will include tests and small docs note. thanks all :clap:
